# The ledger of `ldap`: what a program does with an LDAP directory, measured
# against four clients, each listed on this machine on 2026-11-18. Java's
# `javax.naming.ldap` and `javax.naming.directory` (JNDI's LDAP), from the
# pages of the Java SE 21 API documentation fetched into the scratch folder,
# since no JDK is installed here; .NET's `System.DirectoryServices.Protocols`
# 9.0.0, its members read from the XML documentation of its NuGet package
# fetched into the scratch folder; Go's `github.com/go-ldap/ldap/v3` v3.4.15
# (`go doc -all`, the module fetched into the scratch folder), as the
# yardstick; and Python's `ldap3` 2.9.1 (`dir` of `ldap3`, its `Connection`,
# `Server`, `utils.dn`, `utils.conv`, `utils.uri`, `utils.hashed` and
# `extend`, installed into the scratch folder), as the yardstick. Rust's
# standard library has no LDAP client, so its line is `-` in every block.
#
# Expected values of the tests were observed: the bytes of every request
# from `ldap3`'s own encoder (its operation builders wrapped in an
# `LDAPMessage` and encoded as `ldap3` sends them), a filter's bytes also
# from Go's `CompileFilter`, the parsed and escaped names from Go's `ParseDN`
# and `EscapeDN` and Python's `parse_dn`, `escape_rdn` and
# `escape_filter_chars`, and the server's answers of the loopback server in
# the tests from `ldap3`'s own protocol classes. The live run, on
# 2026-11-18, reached OpenLDAP's slapd 2.6.10 (Debian 13) in a container of
# this Mac: from macOS, StartTLS by `tls.connect_after`; from Linux inside
# the container, StartTLS by `tls.client_over`; and over TLS from the first
# byte, with the certificates of `tls`'s tests.

capability ldap/open  a connection to a directory server opened, in plain text, over TLS from the first byte or with StartTLS
  heroes  ldap.connect, ldap.Conn, ldap.Security
  java    InitialLdapContext, InitialDirContext, LdapContext.newInstance, LdapContext.reconnect
  dotnet  LdapConnection, LdapDirectoryIdentifier, DirectoryIdentifier, DirectoryConnection, LdapSessionOptions.SecureSocketLayer, LdapSessionOptions.ProtocolVersion
  go      Dial, DialTLS, DialURL, DialOpt, DialContext, DialWithDialer, DialWithTLSConfig, DialWithTLSDialer, NewConn, Conn, Conn.Start
  rust    -
  python  Server, Connection, Tls, Connection.open
  note    one connection, which the program owes a `close`; LDAPv3 alone

capability ldap/options  where a server is and how a connection is secured, as a value with defaults
  heroes  ldap.options, ldap.Options, ldap.DEFAULT_PORT, ldap.DEFAULT_TLS_PORT, ldap.DEFAULT_MAX_MESSAGE
  java    Context.PROVIDER_URL, Context.SECURITY_PROTOCOL (environment entries)
  dotnet  LdapSessionOptions.HostName, LdapSessionOptions.DomainName, LdapConnection.Timeout, LdapSessionOptions.SendTimeout
  go      DialContext, Conn.SetTimeout
  rust    -
  python  Server (host, port, use_ssl, connect_timeout), Connection (receive_timeout)
  note    StartTLS by default, the server's certificate checked: a password never crosses in plain text unless the program asks for `.plain`

capability ldap/url  an LDAP URL (RFC 4516) read and written: scheme, host, port, base, attributes, scope, filter, extensions
  heroes  ldap.parse_url, ldap.format_url, ldap.Url, ldap.to_options, ldap.to_query
  java    -
  dotnet  -
  go      DialURL (its scheme and host alone)
  rust    -
  python  utils.uri.parse_uri, utils.uri.unquote
  note    a referral's URLs are read with it; percent escapes decoded, the filter parsed

capability ldap/close  the session ended with an UnbindRequest and the connection closed
  heroes  ldap.close, ldap.is_closed
  java    Context.close
  dotnet  LdapConnection.Dispose
  go      Conn.Unbind, Conn.Close, Conn.IsClosing
  rust    -
  python  Connection.unbind, Connection.closed
  note    -

capability ldap/start-tls  TLS begun on a connection already open (RFC 4511 § 4.14)
  heroes  ldap.start_tls, ldap.Security, ldap.is_secured, ldap.START_TLS
  java    StartTlsRequest, StartTlsResponse, StartTlsResponse.negotiate, StartTlsResponse.close, StartTlsResponse.setHostnameVerifier, StartTlsResponse.setEnabledCipherSuites
  dotnet  LdapSessionOptions.StartTransportLayerSecurity, LdapSessionOptions.StopTransportLayerSecurity, TlsOperationException
  go      Conn.StartTLS, Conn.TLSConnectionState
  rust    -
  python  Connection.start_tls
  note    on Linux and Windows TLS begins over the connection made (`tls.client_over`); on macOS, whose TLS begins only over a connection `tls` makes, `connect` asks for StartTLS on a first connection, reads the answer, and makes the connection it keeps with `tls.connect_after`, expecting the same answer byte for byte (OpenLDAP 2.6.10 answered alike, run 2026-11-18); `start_tls` on an open connection fails `unsupported` there; TLS is never stopped (.NET's Stop)

capability ldap/deadline  each operation bounded in time on the client
  heroes  ldap.set_deadline, ldap.Options
  java    com.sun.jndi.ldap.read.timeout (an environment entry)
  dotnet  LdapConnection.Timeout, DirectoryConnection.Timeout
  go      Conn.SetTimeout
  rust    -
  python  Connection (receive_timeout)
  note    a deadline that passes ends the session, since the next bytes may belong to the late answer

